<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Bale</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1580 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim state of the Bale highlands in southern Ethiopia, a tributary of the Christian emperors and later a battleground with Adal and the Oromo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> appears in 14th-century sources, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a>, as one of the seven Muslim kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Abyssinia</a>. It lay south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shebelle-river">Wabi Shebelle</a> in the highlands that still bear its name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> had spread there through traders and holy men, among them the saint Sheikh Hussein, whose shrine nearby became a major pilgrimage site.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> was a fertile land of grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and horses, governed by local Muslim rulers. It was conquered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> in the 14th century and held as a tributary province, sometimes under appointed governors. In the 15th century it was fought over by the emperors and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Ghazi</a> during his invasion of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in the 1530s. In the later 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> lay close by, expanded across the region, ending the old state. Bale was reincorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> in the 1890s.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1300 CE – 1580 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Horn and East Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:5.57%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1580 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bale</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1440&amp;focus=sultanate-of-bale" aria-label="Sultanate of Bale on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5775 1883 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Bale lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0M6135 2044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6095 2139h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6095 2139h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ethiopia. <a href="/atlas?year=1440&amp;focus=sultanate-of-bale">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

